{
"About a thousand years later Buddhist philosophers turned these attitudes into a theory. Dignāga, in the fifth or sixth century, argued that there are exactly two means of knowledge, perception and inference, because there are exactly two kinds of thing to be known. A short manual of his school, translated into Chinese by Xuanzang, puts it in a line: for ones own understanding, there are only two means of knowledge, perception and inference. Perception is free of conceptual construction, a direct awareness of a particular before any name or class is applied to it. Inference knows its object by way of signs, as one knows fire on a hill from smoke. The rival Nyāya school counted testimony as a third source and comparison as a fourth. The Buddhists held that what we learn from a trustworthy speaker is a case of inference: we reason from his reliability. Dharmakīrti, a century after Dignāga, added the test. A cognition is a means of knowledge if it does not deceive, that is, if the thing it shows is there to do its work when one acts.": "b963c5495be8",
"Buddhist philosophers accepted two sources of knowledge, direct experience and reasoning. Scripture has to answer to both.": "a8327cff3951",
"Faith in Buddhism is like trusting a doctor enough to try the medicine. It is where you start.": "85dbb9bf0488",
"He applied the standard to himself. To the Kālāmas, who were confused by rival teachers, he said not to go by oral transmission, lineage, canonical authority, logic, or the thought the ascetic is our respected teacher, and to rely on what they could know for themselves about what leads to harm and what to welfare. To his own monks he said that an inquirer who cannot read minds should scrutinize the Realized One to understand whether he is a fully awakened Buddha or not, and he described how: watch his conduct over a long time, and see whether anything in it comes from greed, hatred, or delusion. Faith has a place in the path as the confidence that makes a person willing to try. It is expected to be replaced by knowledge.": "929aead875a8",
"He said that faith, tradition, and even careful reasoning can each turn out true or false.": "ce3fe7b8be20",
"Kant agreed with the empiricists that knowledge begins with experience and denied that experience is something simply received. Two faculties are needed, one that is given objects and one that thinks them. Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind. Raw sensation with no concept applied is not yet knowledge of anything. This is aimed at Locke and Hume, and it lands on Dignāgas definition of perception as free of conceptual construction. The Buddhist logicians saw the difficulty themselves. They granted that bare perception does not by itself tell us what we have perceived, and that a conceptual judgment has to follow before we can act. They kept the distinction because it did work for them. It marks the point at which the mind adds names, classes, and mine to what is there, and the path is concerned with exactly that addition. Kant thought the contribution of concepts could not be removed. The Buddhists thought it could be watched happening.": "b27fb7594410",
"On the conclusion Hume is closest: two sources, experience first, no independent weight for scripture. On the test of truth James is closest. Kant is the critic whose objection the Buddhist tradition also raised against itself. Taken together the three show that the Buddhist theory of knowledge sits squarely within what the West calls empiricism, and that it met the standard objections to empiricism in its own history.": "fb3a872a1fff",
"Some things a trained meditator is said to see directly. Until I can, I am relying on someone I have reason to trust, and I should say so.": "55c78c50f2b9",
"The British empiricists held that the mind begins empty and is furnished by experience, and Hume gave the view its sharpest form. All the objects of human reason or enquiry may naturally be divided into two kinds, to wit, Relations of Ideas, and Matters of Fact. The first are known by reasoning alone and the second by experience. A book that contains neither kind of reasoning should be committed to the flames, for it can contain nothing but sophistry and illusion. The two-part structure, the refusal of any third source, and the consequences for religious authority all match Dignāgas position. Hume wrote a famous chapter arguing that testimony about miracles can never outweigh experience, which is the Buddhist treatment of testimony applied to a hard case. The difference is in where each ends. Hume found that experience cannot justify even our belief in causes, and concluded with a mitigated skepticism. Dignāga and Dharmakīrti held that perception and inference, properly used, do yield knowledge, enough to walk a path by.": "bf674bcc01bd",
"The Buddha told his own monks to examine him and see whether he was what he claimed to be.": "262492b723cc",
"The early discourses are unusually explicit about grounds for belief. A young brahmin, Kāpaṭika, told the Buddha that the brahmins hold their scriptures to be the only truth. The Buddha asked whether any of them, back through seven generations of teachers, had claimed to know this for himself, and compared the tradition to a file of blind men, each holding the one in front. He then listed five grounds on which people accept things: Faith, endorsement, oral transmission, reasoned train of thought, and acceptance of a view after deliberation. Each can go either way. Even though you have full faith in something, it may be vacuous, hollow, and false. And even if you dont have full faith in something, it may be true and real. A person who cares about truth may say such is my faith, and may not go on to say this is the only truth, anything else is futile.": "e1c95ff29d00",
"The first further step has been named: the claim that perception is a skill. The discourses say that the Buddhas teaching invites one to come and see, and that it is to be known by the wise each for himself. The logicians gave this a place in the theory. A meditator who has stabilized attention and examined experience closely is said to perceive impermanence and the absence of self in the way that others perceive color. This turns the central doctrines from articles of belief into things that are in principle observable, and it places the burden where the Buddha placed it for the Kālāmas, on ones own knowing. It also creates the traditions hardest problem of evidence. A report from trained observation carries weight only with those who have the training or who have independent grounds for trusting the observer. The discourse on examining the teacher is the traditions answer: judge the observer by what can be seen of his conduct over time.": "a848341de150",
"The likeness ends at two points. The first is what counts as experience. The Buddhist logicians listed four kinds of perception: by the five senses, by the mind, the awareness each mental state has of itself, and the perception of a yogi, which is the direct seeing that comes from long training in attention. The claim is that a trained observer perceives what an untrained one must infer or take on trust, as a physician sees in an X-ray what a layman does not. Hume had no place for this, and a Western empiricist will ask how such perception is to be checked by those who lack it. The second is the purpose. The British empiricists wanted to map the limits of the human understanding. The Buddhists wanted to know which cognitions can be relied on by someone trying to end suffering, since acting on error keeps a person bound. Their epistemology is part of the path. It has the same job as a physicians knowledge of how to tell a true symptom from a false one.": "d0264f769b2f",
"The pragmatists proposed that an idea be judged by what follows when we act on it. For James a true idea is one we can verify, which means that it leads us successfully through experience to what it promised. This is Dharmakīrtis criterion almost word for word. A cognition is a means of knowledge if it is non-deceptive, and it is non-deceptive if the object it presents performs its function: the water one saw can be drunk, the fire one inferred can cook. A mirage fails the test at the moment one bends to drink. Both hold that knowledge is to be assessed by its fruit in practice and without first settling what reality is in itself. They used the criterion for different ends. James used it to defend the right to hold beliefs that make life go better where evidence is lacking. Dharmakīrti used it to exclude them. For him a belief that has not yet met the test is not yet knowledge, however much it helps.": "7a2480364243",
"The second step is that Buddhism did not leave its own theory of knowledge unexamined. Nāgārjuna, three centuries before Dignāga, had asked what establishes the means of knowledge themselves. If they are established by further means, there is a regress. If they establish themselves, any claim might do the same. If they are established by the objects they reveal, the argument goes in a circle. He concluded that means and objects of knowledge depend on each other and that neither is a foundation. Western philosophy calls the same three-way trap by the name of an ancient skeptic and has used it against foundationalism ever since. So the tradition contains both projects. One builds a careful account of what counts as knowing, and the other shows that the account stands on nothing outside itself. Later Buddhist thinkers mostly held the two together, using the theory of knowledge as conventional truth: reliable in practice, like a map, and without a ground beneath it.": "de99dfd43003",
"Their test of knowledge was practical: does it hold up when you act on it?": "35fd89ae8d16",
"Western listeners come with one of two expectations. Those who think of Buddhism as a religion expect to be asked to believe. Those who have read popular books expect to be told that Buddhism is a science and the Buddha an empiricist ahead of his time. The texts support neither without qualification. Begin with the passage in which the Buddha tells his own students to examine him, which surprises both groups. Then describe the position plainly. Faith is where a person starts, as one trusts a doctor enough to try the treatment. The traditions own philosophers held that knowledge comes from direct experience and from reasoning, and that scripture must answer to both. Add the honest limit. Some of what the tradition claims, a trained mind is said to see directly, and until one has that training one is taking a specialists word, with reasons for trusting him.": "d45d6f110594"
}
